<% content_for(:title, "Ship v2 — InstantRecord") %>
<% has_word_count = @columns.include?(Release::COLUMN) %>

<div class="page">
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="micro-label">Demo 03</p>
    <h1>Ship v2</h1>
    <p class="meta">
      Local schema:
      <strong class="data">
        <%= @release_pending ? "v1 · v2 (#{Release.version}) pending" : "v2 (#{Release.version}) applied" %>
      </strong>
    </p>
  </header>

  <p>
    A migration is the one deploy step local-first cannot do for you server-side.
    The database that has to change is sitting in a visitor's browser, holding
    their rows and — if they were offline — a queue of writes that never reached
    you. This page runs that migration for real: DDL and a backfill against the
    local Postgres exactly as you left it.
  </p>

  <ol class="run">
    <li>Write a couple of notes. Each one commits locally and syncs.</li>
    <li>Switch <strong>Offline</strong> on in the inspector, write two more. Those stay in the outbox.</li>
    <li>Read the three numbers below.</li>
    <li>Press <strong>Ship v2</strong>.</li>
    <li>Read them again, and look at the list: the same rows, with the timestamps they already had, now carrying a word count derived from the body text that was already there.</li>
  </ol>

  <section class="state">
    <h2 class="micro-label">The local database right now</h2>

    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>Rows in <code>notes</code></dt>
      <dd class="data"><%= @notes.size %></dd>

      <dt>Outbox rows pending</dt>
      <dd class="data"><%= @pending_count %></dd>

      <dt>Columns in <code>notes</code></dt>
      <dd class="data"><%= @columns.size %></dd>
    </dl>

    <%# Read from the connection, not from Note.column_names — the claim is
        about what the store actually holds, not what the model believes. %>
    <ul class="columns">
      <% @columns.each do |column| %>
        <li class="data <%= "added" if column == Release::COLUMN %>"><%= column %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>

    <% if @release_pending %>
      <p class="change">
        <strong>Ship v2</strong> adds <code><%= Release::COLUMN %></code> to
        <code>notes</code> and backfills it from <code>body</code>. It deletes no
        rows, and it writes no outbox mutations — the backfill runs through a
        migration-local model with <code>update_columns</code>, so nothing
        queues. Both numbers above must read the same afterwards.
      </p>

      <%= form_with url: migrate_ship_path, method: :post,
                    data: { turbo: false, controller: "release", action: "submit->release#ship" } do |f| %>
        <%= f.submit "Ship v2", data: { release_target: "button" } %>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <p class="change">
        v2 applied. <code><%= Release::COLUMN %></code> is in the table above,
        the rows below are the ones you wrote before it ran, and the outbox count
        did not move. The version is recorded in <code>schema_migrations</code>,
        so a reload does not run it again.
      </p>

      <%= form_with url: migrate_ship_path, method: :delete, data: { turbo: false } do |f| %>
        <%= f.submit "Roll back to v1" %>
        <p class="note">
          Drops <code><%= Release::COLUMN %></code> again and leaves every row and
          timestamp alone, so the migration can be watched a second time. The
          migration is an <code>add_column</code> inside <code>change</code>, so
          this is its real reversal — not a separate undo written by hand.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <%= form_with url: migrate_notes_path, method: :post, class: "note-form", data: { turbo: false } do |f| %>
    <%= f.text_field "note[title]", placeholder: "Note title", required: true %>
    <%= f.text_area "note[body]", placeholder: "Body — the word count is derived from this", rows: 2 %>
    <%= f.submit "Write note" %>
  <% end %>

  <table class="notes">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Title</th>
        <th>Body</th>
        <% if has_word_count %><th class="num data"><%= Release::COLUMN %></th><% end %>
        <th>Written</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @notes.each do |note| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= note.title %></td>
          <td class="body"><%= note.body %></td>
          <% if has_word_count %><td class="num data"><%= note.word_count %></td><% end %>
          <td class="data"><%= note.created_at.strftime("%H:%M:%S") %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <section class="scope">
    <h2 class="micro-label">What this does not stage</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>No deploy handover.</strong> The migration runs inside the runtime
      that is already booted. A real deploy installs a new service worker, which
      boots a <em>second</em> Ruby VM against the same PGlite store while the old
      one may still be ticking a sync pass. That double-VM window is a known
      hole and it is not exercised here. Read this page as evidence about DDL
      over live local data, nothing more.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The sync server is still on v1, on purpose.</strong>
      <code>db/schema.rb</code> is one file shared by both runtimes and it is
      what a fresh browser loads at boot, so the only way this browser can be
      genuinely behind is for the server to be behind too. Two consequences,
      both real:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        The mutations you queued before shipping were captured at v1 and drain
        cleanly. The backfilled <code><%= Release::COLUMN %></code> never leaves
        this browser — the server is not told about it.
      </li>
      <li>
        A note written <em>after</em> v2 carries <code><%= Release::COLUMN %></code>
        upward, and the server rejects a column it has never heard of: the batch
        fails and the outbox jams. Watch <code>POST /mutations</code> start
        failing in the inspector. There is no schema negotiation in this
        protocol, so the ordinary rule holds — migrate the server first, ship the
        client second. Opening this page on the sync server itself and pressing
        Ship v2 there is that first half.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>PGlite is inferred, not measured.</strong> The tests behind this
      claim — rows survive, the column is backfilled, the outbox does not move —
      run against server Postgres. PGlite is Postgres compiled to wasm and takes
      the same DDL, but nothing on this page proves that independently.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
